As per the current implementation we use one rate per storage type, charges per box per day and count days from receiving the crop consigment to return with a one-day minimum.

Q1. Is the container list exactly half box, full box, crate, or should we build it as a extendable master list(quarter box, sack, bin, tray etc)?

Q2. Are the same container types accepted in all four storage types (CA, MA, CS, IQF), or does each type accept only some of them?

Q3. Does the available container type depend on the crop as well — apples in boxes, eggs in crates — or is any container type valid for any crop?

Q4. Can a single consignment hold a mix of container types — say 20 full boxes, 10 half boxes and 5 crates brought together by one depositor — or is one consignment always a single container type, with a mixed delivery entered as separate consignments?

Q5. Currently operator enters weight per box and may override the computed total. Now if a mix is allowed, will the operator enter weight per container type for every type?

Q6. Today a facility is configured as blocks × racks per storage type and one rack holds exactly one consignment. Does that still hold? Or does a rack now have a capacity expressed in container — a rack takes 20 full boxes, or 40 half boxes, or mix?

Q7. Today one rate is held per storage type, effective from a date, and a consignment is charged at the rate applicable on the day it was received. Now with container type(full box, half box, crate etc) the new rate is defined against storage type + container type + effective date. With 4 storage types and 3 container types, we need to have12 rates at a time per facility?

Q8. Will the rate also vary by crop? Adding crop would multiply the number of rates to maintain.

Q9. Must the rate for every container type be defined before a storage type can accept a crop? Or storage type can be used for some container types e.g. only full boxes priced, in that case we refuse a half-box intake.

Q10. Is the minimum period is only for billing and the depositor may collect whenever they like, but is charged for at least minimum period — or a lock-in, where the system refuses return before minimum period has passed?

Q11. Is the minimum period per storage type only, or does it also vary by crop or by container type?

Q12. How is the minimum amount charged — as N days × the daily rate, or as a separate flat amount that has nothing to do with the daily rate? For example ₹100 per box for the first 30 days, then ₹5 per box per day after that.

Q13. What happens when the minimum period is longer than the crop's shelf life — for instance a 45-day minimum on a crop with 30 days of life? Should intake be refused, or allowed with a warning?

Q14. On the return slip and the payment receipt, should the charge be itemised — a line per container type with quantity, rate and amount — or shown as a single total?

Q15. Should the minimum-period portion appear as its own line, separate from the per-day portion in return slip? A depositor collecting on day 40 would then see the minimum charge and the ten extra days as two lines.

Q16. In reports and on the monitoring dashboard, should stored quantity be shown as container types or as weight? As per current implementation it is boxes and tonnes.
